# ── Single-host deployment ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE hostname (`localhost`) fronts janux's full surface AND a protected
# static resource at `/app`, both behind forward-auth.
#
# TLS is `tls internal` below (per site): Caddy signs `localhost` with its
# local CA. NOTE: this Caddy build ignores the `CADDY_TLS` env var and its
# caddyfile adapter rejects a `global { tls internal }` block, so the
# directive lives in the site block -- verified not to interfere with the
# `handle` blocks below.
#
# ── Why TLS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The janux session cookie is `Secure` (+ host-only, no Domain attribute; see
# src/verify.rs::set_session_cookie), so it only transmits over HTTPS.
# `localhost` is a browser secure-context, so a local CA is the clean
# choice; on macOS trust that CA once with `up.py single-host trust-ca`
# (up.py handles the /etc/hosts mapping only for split-hosts).
#
# ── Request flow ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#      /api/v1/auth/verify -> janux     (public — Caddy POSTs the auth probe here)
#      /app                 -> forward_auth janux verify -> nginx (gated)
#      /   (everything else) -> janux      (discovery, OIDC, admin UI, SCIM,
#                                          health, the /login page that
#                                          redirects land on)
#
# ── The redirect on unauth ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# When a gate probe is UNAUTHENTICATED, janux answers it with a 303 -> /login
# because `forward_auth_redirect = true` in base.toml. Caddy's `forward_auth`
# simply propagates that non-2xx to the browser, which then authenticates at
# /login, gets its host-only cookie, and retries /app. The redirect lives on
# the AUTH side (janux knows its login URL), so it works for Caddy AND nginx
# `auth_request` alike — a proxy cannot inject a Location into a copied 3xx.
# (Off by default; with it off janux answers a bare 401 problem+json, also a
# valid "denied" result — the gate still blocks the request, just no bounce.)

localhost {
	# Sign localhost with caddy's local CA so browsers get a closed TLS
	# chain. This per-site form is what this caddy build accepts.
	tls internal

	# The demo "service": a static file gated by forward-auth. A
	# successful (2xx) probe falls through to nginx; an unauthenticated
	# probe yields janux's 303 -> /login, propagated below.
	@appName path /app
	handle @appName {
		forward_auth auth:8080 {
			uri /api/v1/auth/verify
				# janux runs its DEFAULT-DENY engine on the PROXIED request: it reads
				# X-Forwarded-{Host,Uri,Method} (Caddy sets them) and evaluates the
				# tenant's policy for resource "/app" (seeded in seed.toml). 200 ->
				# allow; 401 -> unauth; 403 -> the who/is/for triple lacks permission.
			}
		reverse_proxy app:80
	}

	# Everything else is the janux auth server + hosted UI — all public, so
	# /login and the admin console are reachable.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy auth:8080
	}
}
